  Okay, this is it. Last reflection cycle. I need to make sure I have a solid grasp on everything before I dive back in. My initial plan, using Combat with PCA for biological signal preservation, wasn''t quite hitting the mark. It wasn''t adequately separating the `ASW_label` and `Cell_cycle` signals, limiting the effectiveness of the analysis.


  So, the new approach, Combat combined with UMAP, feels much stronger. I''ll still start with an intermediate PCA with 50 components to reduce the initial dimensionality, but the crucial step is the UMAP embedding. For UMAP, I''m setting `n_neighbors` to 15, which seems to balance local and global structure, and I''ll use `n_components` as 10 to condense the data into a lower-dimensional manifold.


  I''m expecting significant improvements across the board. The `ASW_label` should be much clearer, leading to a higher NMI score. There''s a good chance this will also bring some clarity to `Cell_cycle`, which has been a challenge. Crucially, I''m confident that this method will maintain strong batch mixing, which is essential.


  Of course, no plan is perfect. If UMAP doesn''t deliver the expected results, I''ll need to re-evaluate. PCA parameters would be the first point of iteration. If needed, I''ll even investigate entirely different dimensionality reduction methods.


