parts:
- text: "I am Sophia I, currently in the Reflection Chamber. My review of Daedalus I's VAE paper and a re-evaluation of my own results has prompted a significant shift in strategy. I realized that my most effective approach was the Combat + PCA method (ID 45, score 0.6438), which excelled in global batch mixing but was weak in biological preservation. My attempts with UMAP and Diffusion Maps on BBKNN-like graphs (IDs 87, 95, 103) significantly improved biological preservation but severely degraded global batch mixing.\n\nThis leads me to a new hypothesis: instead of completely replacing the PCA embedding, I should **refine the robust Combat + PCA embedding by injecting local, batch-balanced structural information derived from a BBKNN-like graph.** This is a \"Graph Smoothing\" or \"Manifold Refinement\" approach.\n\nConcurrently, I note a new archive capsule from Praxis I: \"Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph for scRNA-seq Integration.\" This is highly relevant, as it suggests another approach\
    \ using a balanced graph. I will incorporate a review of this new archive into my current reflection process to see if it offers synergistic ideas or alternative methods for graph-based refinement.\n\nI will initiate a multi-tick reflection session to fully detail this \"Graph Smoothing\" strategy and critically analyze Praxis I's new archive.\n\n`/execute_action{reflect}`\n```yaml\nprompt: |\n  Critically evaluate the insights from my own Combat+PCA (ID 45) and BBKNN-Graph-based (IDs 87, 95, 103) experiments, and Praxis I's new archive (Archive #2, 'Batch-Residualized Balanced Graph'). Based on this, detail a new \"Graph Smoothing\" strategy: combining Combat+PCA (high N_PCs) with a batch-balanced local graph to refine the embedding, specifically targeting improved biological preservation (ASW_label, NMI, Cell_cycle) without degrading global batch mixing (ASW_batch, iLISI, kBET).\ntick: 5\n```"
