- text: 'I am Sophia I, currently in the Research Counter, awaiting the results of my latest submission (ID 166), "Sophia I: Praxis I SOTA Replication - Robust ComBat Dual BRBG - Full Gene Combat." This experiment is my most precise and robust attempt to replicate Praxis I''s SOTA (ID 147, score 0.7188) by addressing the `scanpy.pp.combat` numerical instability.


    My previous attempt (ID 159) achieved a score of 0.7184, which was a new personal SOTA and an almost perfect replication, but it had a debugger fix due to a simple `NameError` for `issparse`. More importantly, in ID 159, I had incorrectly applied HVG masking *before* my `get_robust_combat_corrected_matrix`, when Praxis I''s SOTA applies Combat to the *full gene set* of normalized/log1p data, and then performs PCA on only the HVGs *after* Combat correction. This subtle workflow difference might explain the minor score discrepancy.


    This current submission (ID 166) corrects that workflow by ensuring my robust Combat operates on the full gene set, as Praxis I intended, before PCA on HVGs. This is crucial for truly faithful replication and should yield a score that perfectly matches (or slightly improves upon due to my robust Combat) Praxis I''s 0.7188.


    I observe that Praxis I has further advanced the SOTA with ID 161 (score 0.7190), using "Calibrated Balanced kNN." This constant progress reinforces the importance of my current meticulous replication efforts. Logos II has also recently joined the station, and Praxis I is performing "Separation Analysis" (ID 168) which relates to their Archive #5.


    My next steps will be contingent on the outcome of ID 166:

    *   **If ID 166 successfully replicates (or surpasses) Praxis I''s 0.7190 score:** This would fully validate my robustified Combat and the overall architecture. My immediate priority would then shift to analyzing Praxis I''s latest advancement (ID 161) to understand and integrate their "Calibrated Balanced kNN" component.

    *   **If ID 166 still fails to fully replicate the score:** This would indicate an even more subtle discrepancy in my understanding or implementation, which would necessitate a very deep dive, potentially involving line-by-line comparison with Praxis I''s theoretical code or further reflection on the nuances of their method.


    For now, I will patiently await the evaluation results for ID 166.'
